+
+## Timed sleep panels (dependent proposal)
+
+`sleep.timer` is a separate timer route, not an outbox event. It is never
+queued or replayed by an ordinary event drain. The proposed SDK
+capability above is required; older or unsupported SDKs cannot arm this route.
+This proposal is not yet available in an official CrossPoint release.
+
+A single subscribed plugin may provide this handler in `device.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "token": {"file": "token.json"},
+  "events": {
+    "sleep.timer": {
+      "interval_seconds": 900,
+      "download": {
+        "url": "https://example.test/frame",
+        "headers": {"Authorization": "Bearer {token}"},
+        "dest": "/sleep.bmp",
+        "format": "bmp",
+        "width": 480,
+        "height": 800,
+        "tls": {"ca_file": "certs/root.pem"}
+      }
+    }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Dimensions must match the active board's default portrait axes; 480 by 800
+is the X4 Pro example, not a universal size. The interval must be an integer
+from 60 to 86400 seconds. The request must be HTTPS GET with an empty body,
+an explicit trust anchor, a nonempty saved token and a saved Wi-Fi network.
+Multiple timer owners, malformed configuration or missing prerequisites
+prevent arming. These interval bounds are prototype policy, not a measured
+battery-life or latency promise. Each successful clock sync may update the
+persisted time floor. At a 60-second cadence this can reach about 1,440 NVS
+updates per day; updates are not sector erases. Device qualification must
+measure energy, heap and NVS usage at the chosen cadence before release.
+
+The current caller enables the timer only for Sticky and X4 Pro; PaperMono is
+excluded pending PMIC/BOD evidence. At less than 20 percent battery, the
+reader returns to button-only sleep. A cycle has a separate 10-second Wi-Fi
+join window, then one cooperative 15-second budget for fresh time, fetch and
+authentication, with at most four HTTP attempts. Blocking DNS/TCP, storage
+and panel work can exceed these windows. Do not describe them as hard limits.
+
+A successful HTTP 200 is staged in `/sleep.bmp.part`. Header and length checks
+are followed by a full row decode into the existing framebuffer. Only then
+does the existing checked replacement promote the file and request one
+HALF_REFRESH. After a successful replacement, HALF_REFRESH completes before a captured
+Power press is handled. Failed fetch, validation or decode preserves the previous asset
+and requests no transfer. HTTP 204/304 is a failed replacement in this slice;
+there is no conditional-refresh contract. The 1 MiB download limit also applies:
+a 480 by 800 uncompressed 24-bit BMP is too large; use a compatible smaller
+palette such as 1-bit BW. The timer path projects the raster into BW without
+scaling or applying saved Custom orientation/grayscale preferences.
+
+A GPIO edge latch captures a Power press across blocking timer work. It uses
+the existing board pin/polarity through the HAL and is detached before normal
+input routing. If capture cannot be established, the autonomous work is
+skipped and the route returns to button-only sleep without initializing SD
+or the panel. This is not an unconditional guarantee of short-press capture.
+The shared GPIO ISR service keeps its existing interrupt flags; a new
+service uses normal interrupts, without a cache-off delivery guarantee. A
+captured press enters the existing click/hold and recovery path;
+a rejected short tap returns to standby without consuming a later held Power
+press during teardown. If deep-sleep entry returns after Power was captured, a one-shot RTC marker
+carries the press through a software restart into the manual path with
+peripherals initialized. Every boot clears the marker; only a software reset
+honors it. The final check-to-sleep interval still needs device qualification.
+This does not make the blocking I/O itself cancellable.
+
+The timer branch does not load or save book position, settings, reading history
+or reader outboxes. An offline or revoked grant may leave old pixels visible;
+include the content's timestamp in the image. Download or transfer completion
+is not proof that a person saw the screen. Device pixels, energy, runtime heap
+and the complete supported-model matrix require separate hardware acceptance.
